#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:60
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:62
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kwrite; is more than a text editor for the &kde; Desktop. It is meant to be "
"a programmer's editor, and could be considered as at least a partial "
"alternative to more powerful editors. It may be best used in conjunction "
"with &konqueror; for source file browsing for different languages. &kwrite; "
"also works very well as a simple text editor. One of &kwrite;'s main "
"features is the colorized syntax, customized for many different programming "
"languages such as: C/C++, &Java;, Python, Perl, Bash, Modula 2, &HTML;, and "
"Ada."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid "Some Fundamentals"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kwrite; is very simple to use. Anyone that has used a text editor should "
"have no problems."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:84
#, no-c-format
msgid "Drag and Drop"
msgstr "Rossegar e pausar"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kwrite; uses the &kde; Drag and Drop protocol. Files may be dragged and "
"dropped onto &kwrite; from the Desktop, &konqueror; or some remote &FTP; "
"site opened in one of &konqueror;'s windows."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid "Command Line Options"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Though &kwrite; may most often be started from the &kde; program menu, or a "
"desktop icon, it can also be opened at the command line prompt of a terminal "
"window. There are a few useful options that are available when doing this."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid "Specify a File"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"By specifying the path and name of a particular file the user can have "
"&kwrite; open (or create) that file immediately upon startup. This option "
"might look something like the following:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<prompt>%</prompt> <userinput><command>kwrite</command>\n"
"<option><replaceable>/home/myhome/docs/myfile.txt</replaceable></option>\n"
"</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid "Specify a file on the internet"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The above-mentioned method could even be used to open files on the internet "
"(if the user has an active connection at the time.) An example of this might "
"look like the following:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:120
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<prompt>%</prompt> <userinput><command>kwrite</command>\n"
"<option><replaceable>ftp://ftp.kde.org/pub/kde/Welcome.msg</replaceable>\n"
"</option></userinput>"
msgstr ""

